The proof revolves around recognizing that a typical entry of the product $\adjoint{A}A$ is an inner product of columns of $A$. Here are the details to support this claim.

$$\begin{aligned}\matrixentry{\adjoint{A}A}{ij}
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{\adjoint{A}}{ik}\matrixentry{A}{kj}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{\transpose{\conjugate{A}}}{ik}\matrixentry{A}{kj}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{\,\conjugate{A}\,}{ki}\matrixentry{A}{kj}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\conjugate{\matrixentry{A}{ki}}\matrixentry{A}{kj}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\conjugate{\vectorentry{\vect{A}_i}{k}}\vectorentry{\vect{A}_j}{k}\\
&=\innerproduct{\vect{A}_i}{\vect{A}_j}
&&\end{aligned}$$

We now employ this equality in a chain of equivalences,

$$\begin{aligned}&\text{$S=\set{\vectorlist{A}{n}}$ is an orthonormal set}\\
&\iff \innerproduct{\vect{A}_i}{\vect{A}_j}=
\begin{cases}
0 &\text{if $i\neq j$}\\
1 &\text{if $i=j$}
\end{cases}&&\\
&\iff \matrixentry{\adjoint{A}A}{ij}=
\begin{cases}
0 &\text{if $i\neq j$}\\
1 &\text{if $i=j$}
\end{cases}\\
&\iff \matrixentry{\adjoint{A}A}{ij}=\matrixentry{I_n}{ij},\ 1\leq i\leq n,\ 1\leq j\leq n
&&\\
&\iff \adjoint{A}A=I_n
&&\\
&\iff \text{$A$ is a unitary matrix}
&&\end{aligned}$$

The matrix

$$U=
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{1 + i }{{\sqrt{5}}} &
 \frac{3 + 2\,i }{{\sqrt{55}}} &
 \frac{2+2i}{\sqrt{22}} \\
\frac{1 - i }{{\sqrt{5}}} &
 \frac{2 + 2\,i }{{\sqrt{55}}} &
 \frac{-3 + i }{{\sqrt{22}}} \\
\frac{i }{{\sqrt{5}}} &
 \frac{3 - 5\,i }{{\sqrt{55}}} &
 -\frac{2}{\sqrt{22}}
\end{bmatrix}$$

from [example UM3] is a unitary matrix. By [theorem CUMOS], its columns

$$\set{
\colvector{
\frac{1 + i }{{\sqrt{5}}}\\
 \frac{1 - i }{{\sqrt{5}}}\\
 \frac{i }{{\sqrt{5}}}
},\,
\colvector{
\frac{3 + 2\,i }{{\sqrt{55}}}\\
 \frac{2 + 2\,i }{{\sqrt{55}}}\\
 \frac{3 - 5\,i }{{\sqrt{55}}}
},\,
\colvector{
\frac{2+2i}{\sqrt{22}}\\
 \frac{-3 + i }{{\sqrt{22}}}\\
 -\frac{2}{\sqrt{22}}
}
}$$

form an orthonormal set. You might find checking the six inner products of pairs of these vectors easier than doing the matrix product $\adjoint{U}U$. Or, because the inner product is anti-commutative ([theorem IPAC]) you only need check three inner products (see [exercise MINM.T12]).

Suppose that $U$ is a unitary matrix of size $n$ and $\vect{u}$ and $\vect{v}$ are two vectors from $\complex{n}$. Then

$$\begin{aligned}\innerproduct{U\vect{u}}{U\vect{v}}&=\innerproduct{\vect{u}}{\vect{v}}
&
&\text{and}
&
\norm{U\vect{v}}&=\norm{\vect{v}}\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}\innerproduct{U\vect{u}}{U\vect{v}}
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{U\vect{u}}\right)}U\vect{v}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{U}\conjugate{\vect{u}}\right)}U\vect{v}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}\transpose{\conjugate{U}}U\vect{v}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}\adjoint{U}U\vect{v}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}I_n\vect{v}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}\vect{v}
&&\\
&=\innerproduct{\vect{u}}{\vect{v}}
&&\end{aligned}$$

The second conclusion is just a specialization of the first conclusion.

$$\begin{aligned}\norm{U\vect{v}}
&=\sqrt{\norm{U\vect{v}}^2}\\
&=\sqrt{\innerproduct{U\vect{v}}{U\vect{v}}}&&\\
&=\sqrt{\innerproduct{\vect{v}}{\vect{v}}}\\
&=\sqrt{\norm{\vect{v}}^2}&&\\
&=\norm{\vect{v}}\end{aligned}$$

Assume that $ad-bc\neq 0$. We will use the definition of the inverse of a matrix to establish that $A$ has an inverse ([definition MI]). Note that if $ad-bc\neq 0$ then the displayed formula for $\inverse{A}$ is legitimate since we are not dividing by zero). Using this proposed formula for the inverse of $A$, we compute

$$\begin{aligned}A\inverse{A}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}
\left(\frac{1}{ad-bc}
\begin{bmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{bmatrix}
\right)
=
\frac{1}{ad-bc}
\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc&0\\0&ad-bc\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}\\
and
\inverse{A}A
&=
\frac{1}{ad-bc}
\begin{bmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{ad-bc}
\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc&0\\0&ad-bc\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}$$

By [definition MI] this is sufficient to establish that $A$ is invertible, and that the expression for $\inverse{A}$ is correct.

 Assume that $A$ is invertible, and proceed with a proof by contradiction ([technique CD]), by assuming also that $ad-bc=0$. This translates to $ad=bc$. Let

$$B=
\begin{bmatrix}
e&f\\
g&h
\end{bmatrix}$$

be a putative inverse of $A$.

This means that

$$I_2=AB=
\begin{bmatrix}
a&b\\
c&d
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
e&f\\
g&h
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
ae+bg & af+bh\\
ce+dg & cf+dh
\end{bmatrix}$$

Working on the matrices on two ends of this equation, we will multiply the top row by $c$ and the bottom row by $a$.

$$\begin{bmatrix}
c&0\\
0&a
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
ace+bcg & acf+bch\\
ace+adg & acf+adh
\end{bmatrix}$$

We are assuming that $ad=bc$, so we can replace two occurrences of $ad$ by $bc$ in the bottom row of the right matrix.

$$\begin{bmatrix}
c&0\\
0&a
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
ace+bcg & acf+bch\\
ace+bcg & acf+bch
\end{bmatrix}$$

The matrix on the right now has two rows that are identical, and therefore the same must be true of the matrix on the left. Identical rows for the matrix on the left implies that $a=0$ and $c=0$.

With this information, the product $AB$ becomes

$$\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
=I_2
=AB
=
\begin{bmatrix}
ae+bg & af+bh\\
ce+dg & cf+dh
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
bg & bh\\
dg & dh
\end{bmatrix}$$

So $bg=dh=1$ and thus $b,g,d,h$ are all nonzero. But then $bh$ and $dg$ (the other corners) must also be nonzero, so this is (finally) a contradiction. So our assumption was false and we see that $ad-bc\neq 0$ whenever $A$ has an inverse.

A matrix equality, so we will go entry-by-entry, no surprise there. For $1\leq i\leq m$, $1\leq j\leq s$,

$$\begin{aligned}\matrixentry{A(BD)}{ij}
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{BD}{kj}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\left(\sum_{\ell=1}^{p}\matrixentry{B}{k\ell}\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}\right)
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{\ell=1}^{p}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{B}{k\ell}\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}
&&\\
We can switch the order of the summation since these are finite sums,
&=\sum_{\ell=1}^{p}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{B}{k\ell}\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}
&&\\
As $\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}$ does not depend on the index $k$, we can use distributivity to move it outside of the inner sum,
&=\sum_{\ell=1}^{p}\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{B}{k\ell}\right)
&&\\
&=\sum_{\ell=1}^{p}\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}\matrixentry{AB}{i\ell}
&&\\
&=\sum_{\ell=1}^{p}\matrixentry{AB}{i\ell}\matrixentry{D}{\ell j}
&&\\
&=\matrixentry{(AB)D}{ij}&&\end{aligned}$$

So the matrices $(AB)D$ and $A(BD)$ are equal, entry-by-entry, and by the definition of matrix equality ([definition ME]) we can say they are equal matrices.

If we consider the vectors $\vect{u},\,\vect{v}\in\complex{m}$ as $m\times 1$ matrices then

$$\begin{aligned}\innerproduct{\vect{u}}{\vect{v}}
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}\vect{v}
=\adjoint{\vect{u}}\vect{v}\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}\innerproduct{\vect{u}}{\vect{v}}
&=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\conjugate{\vectorentry{\vect{u}}{k}}\vectorentry{\vect{v}}{k}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\conjugate{\matrixentry{\vect{u}}{k1}}\matrixentry{\vect{v}}{k1}
&&\text{Column vectors as matrices}\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\matrixentry{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}{k1}\matrixentry{\vect{v}}{k1}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\matrixentry{\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}}{1k}\matrixentry{\vect{v}}{k1}
&&\\
&=\matrixentry{\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}\vect{v}}{11}
&&\\\end{aligned}$$

To finish we just blur the distinction between a $1\times 1$ matrix ($\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{u}}}\vect{v}$) and its lone entry.

To obtain this matrix equality, we will work entry-by-entry. For $1\leq i\leq m$, $1\leq j\leq p$,

$$\begin{aligned}\matrixentry{\conjugate{AB}}{ij}
&=\conjugate{\matrixentry{AB}{ij}}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{B}{kj}}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\conjugate{\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{B}{kj}}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\conjugate{\matrixentry{A}{ik}}\,\,\conjugate{\matrixentry{B}{kj}}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{\conjugate{A}}{ik}\matrixentry{\conjugate{B}}{kj}
&&\\
&=\matrixentry{\conjugate{A}\,\conjugate{B}}{ij}
&&\end{aligned}$$

So the matrices $\conjugate{AB}$ and $\conjugate{A}\,\conjugate{B}$ are equal, entry-by-entry, and by the definition of matrix equality ([definition ME]) we can say they are equal matrices.

This theorem may be surprising but if we check the sizes of the matrices involved, then maybe it will not seem so far-fetched. First, $AB$ has size $m\times p$, so its transpose has size $p\times m$. The product of $\transpose{B}$ with $\transpose{A}$ is a $p\times n$ matrix times an $n\times m$ matrix, also resulting in a $p\times m$ matrix. So at least our objects are compatible for equality (and would not be, in general, if we did not reverse the order of the matrix multiplication).

Here we go again, entry-by-entry. For $1\leq i\leq m$, $1\leq j\leq p$,

$$\begin{aligned}\matrixentry{\transpose{(AB)}}{ji}=&\matrixentry{AB}{ij}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{A}{ik}\matrixentry{B}{kj}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{B}{kj}\matrixentry{A}{ik}
&&\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\matrixentry{\transpose{B}}{jk}\matrixentry{\transpose{A}}{ki}
&&\\
&=\matrixentry{\transpose{B}\transpose{A}}{ji}
&&\end{aligned}$$

So the matrices $\transpose{(AB)}$ and $\transpose{B}\transpose{A}$ are equal, entry-by-entry, and by the definition of matrix equality ([definition ME]) we can say they are equal matrices.

$$\begin{aligned}\innerproduct{A\vect{x}}{\vect{y}}
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A\vect{x}}\right)}\vect{y}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\,\conjugate{\vect{x}}\right)}\vect{y}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{x}}}\transpose{\conjugate{A}}\vect{y}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\conjugate{\vect{x}}}\left(\adjoint{A}\vect{y}\right)
&&\\
&=\innerproduct{\vect{x}}{\adjoint{A}\vect{y}}
&&\end{aligned}$$

For $1\leq i\leq m$, $1\leq j\leq n$,

$$\begin{aligned}\matrixentry{\conjugate{\left(\transpose{A}\right)}}{ji}
&=\conjugate{\matrixentry{\transpose{A}}{ji}}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\matrixentry{A}{ij}}
&&\\
&=\matrixentry{\conjugate{A}}{ij}
&&\\
&=\matrixentry{\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}}{ji}
&&\\\end{aligned}$$

Since the matrices $\conjugate{\left(\transpose{A}\right)}$ and $\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}$ are equal in each entry, [definition ME] says that $\conjugate{\left(\transpose{A}\right)}=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}$.

$$\begin{aligned}\adjoint{\left(A+B\right)}
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A+B}\right)}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}+\conjugate{B}\right)}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}+\transpose{\left(\conjugate{B}\right)}
&&\\
&=\adjoint{A}+\adjoint{B}
&&\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}\adjoint{\left(\alpha A\right)}
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{\alpha A}\right)}
&&\\
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{\alpha}\conjugate{A}\right)}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\alpha}\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\alpha}\adjoint{A}
&&\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}\adjoint{\left(\adjoint{A}\right)}
&=\transpose{\left(\conjugate{\left(\adjoint{A}\right)}\right)}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\left(\transpose{\left(\adjoint{A}\right)}\right)}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\left(\transpose{\left(\transpose{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}\right)}\right)}
&&\\
&=\conjugate{\left(\conjugate{A}\right)}
&&\\
&=A
&&\end{aligned}$$

Consider the linear transformation, $\ltdefn{S}{P_3}{M_{22}}$, given by

