\Tr(A)Trace of a matrix

 The trace of a square matrix $A$ is the number $\Tr(A)$ obtained by summing the diagonal entries of $A$:

$$\def\r{\color{red}}
 \Tr\mat{\r a_{11} a_{12} \cdots, a_{1,n-1} a_{1n} ;
 a_{21} \r a_{22} \cdots, a_{2,n-1} a_{2n} ;
 \vdots, \vdots, \ddots, \vdots, \vdots ;
 a_{n-1,1} a_{n-1,2} \cdots, \r a_{n-1,n-1} a_{n-1,n} ;
 a_{n1} a_{n2} \cdots, a_{n,n-1} \r a_{nn}}
 =
 \r a_{11} + a_{22} + \cdots + a_{nn}\color{black}.$$

Find the complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix

$$A = \mat{1 -1; 1 1}.$$

 The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is

$$f(\lambda) = \lambda^2 - \Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A)
 = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 2.$$

 The roots of this polynomial are

$$\lambda = \frac{2\pm\sqrt{4-8}}2 = 1\pm i.$$

 First we compute an eigenvector for $\lambda = 1+i$. We have

$$A-(1+i) I_2 = \mat{1-(1+i) -1; 1 1-(1+i)} = \mat{-i -1; 1 -i}.$$

 Now we row reduce, noting that the second row is $i$ times the first:

$$\mat{-i -1; 1 -i}
 \;\xrightarrow{R_2=R_2-iR_1}\;
 \mat{-i -1; 0 0}
 \;\xrightarrow{R_1=R_1\div -i}\;
 \mat{1 -i; 0 0}.$$

 The parametric form is $x = iy$, so that an eigenvector is $v_1={i\choose 1}$. Next we compute an eigenvector for $\lambda=1-i$. We have

$$A-(1-i) I_2 = \mat{1-(1-i) -1; 1 1-(1-i)} = \mat{i -1; 1 i}.$$

 Now we row reduce, noting that the second row is $-i$ times the first:

$$\mat{i -1; 1 i}
 \;\xrightarrow{R_2=R_2+iR_1}\;
 \mat{i -1; 0 0}
 \;\xrightarrow{R_1=R_1\div i}\;
 \mat{1 i; 0 0}.$$

 The parametric form is $x = -iy$, so that an eigenvector is $v_2 = {-i\choose 1}$.

 We can verify our answers:

$$\begin{split}
 \mat{1 -1; 1 1}\vec{i 1} \amp= \vec{i-1 i+1} = (1+i)\vec{i 1} \\
 \mat{1 -1; 1 1}\vec{-i 1} \amp= \vec{-i-1 -i+1} = (1-i)\vec{-i 1}.
 \end{split}$$

Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors, real and complex, of the matrix

$$A = \mat{4/5 -3/5 0; 3/5 4/5 0; 1 2 2}.$$

 We compute the characteristic polynomial by expanding cofactors along the third row:

$$f(\lambda) = \det\mat{4/5-\lambda, -3/5 0; 3/5 4-5-\lambda, 0; 1 2 2-\lambda}
 = (2-\lambda)\left(\lambda^2-\frac 85\lambda+1\right).$$

 This polynomial has one real root at $2$, and two complex roots at

$$\lambda = \frac{8/5\pm\sqrt{64/25-4}}2 = \frac{4\pm 3i}5.$$

 Therefore, the eigenvalues are

$$\lambda = 2,\quad \frac{4+3i}5,\quad \frac{4-3i}5.$$

 We eyeball that $v_1 = e_3$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $2$, since the third column is $2e_3$.

 Next we find an eigenvector with eigenvaluue $(4+3i)/5$. We have

$$A-\frac{4+3i}5I_3 =
\mat{-3i/5 -3/5 0; 3/5 -3i/5 0;
1 2 2-(4+3i)/5}
\;\xrightarrow[R_2=R_2\times5/3]{R_1=R_1\times -5/3}\;
\mat{i 1 0; 1 -i 0; 1 2 \frac{6-3i}5}.$$

 We row reduce, noting that the second row is $-i$ times the first:

$$\begin{split}
 \mat{i 1 0; 1 -i 0; 1 2 \frac{6-3i}5}
 \amp\;\xrightarrow[\hbox to 2cm{}]{R_2=R_2+iR_1}\;
 \mat{i 1 0; 0 0 0; 1 2 \frac{6-3i}5} \\
 \amp\;\xrightarrow[\hbox to 2cm{}]{R_3=R_3+iR_1}\;
 \mat{i 1 0; 0 0 0; 0 2+i \frac{6-3i}5} \\
 \amp\;\xrightarrow[\hbox to 2cm{}]{R_2\longleftrightarrow R_3}\;
 \mat{i 1 0; 0 2+i \frac{6-3i}5; 0 0 0} \\
 \amp\;\xrightarrow[\hbox to 2cm{\tiny\hss$R_2=R_2\div (2+i)$\hss}]{R_1=R_1\div i}\;
 \mat{1 -i 0; 0 1 \frac{9-12i}{25}; 0 0 0} \\
 \amp\;\xrightarrow[\hbox to 2cm{}]{R_1=R_1+iR_2}\;
 \mat{1 0 \frac{12+9i}{25}; 0 1 \frac{9-12i}{25}; 0 0 0}.
 \end{split}$$

 The free variable is $z$; the parametric form of the solution is

$$\syseq{
 x = -\dfrac{12+9i}{25}z;
 y = -\dfrac{9-12i}{25}z\rlap.
 }$$

 Taking $z=25$ gives the eigenvector

$$v_2 = \vec{-12-9i -9+12i 25}.$$

 A similar calculation (replacing all occurences of $i$ by $-i$) shows that an eigenvector with eigenvalue $(4-3i)/5$ is

$$v_3 = \vec{-12+9i -9-12i 25}.$$

 We can verify our calculations:

$$\begin{split}
 \mat{4/5 -3/5 0; 3/5 4/5 0; 1 2 2}
 \vec{-12+9i -9-12i 25} \amp= \vec{-21/5+72i/5 -72/5-21i/5, 20-15i}
 = \frac{4+3i}5\vec{-12+9i -9-12i 25} \\
 \mat{4/5 -3/5 0; 3/5 4/5 0; 1 2 2}
 \vec{-12-9i -9+12i 25} \amp= \vec{-21/5-72i/5 -72/5+21i/5, 20+15i}
 = \frac{4-3i}5\vec{-12-9i -9+12i 25}. \\
 \end{split}$$

The most important examples of matrices with complex eigenvalues are rotation-scaling matrices, i.e., scalar multiples of rotation matrices.

 The following proposition justifies the name.

 Geometrically, a rotation-scaling matrix does exactly what the name says: it rotates and scales (in either order).

 The matrix in the second example has second column ${-\sqrt 3\choose 1}$, which is rotated counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis by an angle of $5\pi/6$. This rotation angle is not equal to
 $\tan\inv\bigl(1/(-\sqrt3)\bigr) = -\frac\pi 6.$
 The problem is that arctan always outputs values between $-\pi/2$ and $\pi/2$: it does not account for points in the second or third quadrants. This is why we drew a triangle and used its (positive) edge lengths to compute the angle $\varphi$:

