Let $A$ be a $2\times 2$ real matrix with a complex (non-real) eigenvalue $\lambda$, and let $v$ be an eigenvector. Then $A = CBC\inv$ for

$$C = \mat{| |; \Re(v) \Im(v); | |}
 \sptxt{and}
 B = \mat{\Re(\lambda) \Im(\lambda); -\Im(\lambda) \Re(\lambda)}.$$

 In particular, $A$ is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix that scales by a factor of $|\lambda| = \sqrt{\det(B)}.$

What does the matrix

$$A = \mat{2 -1; 2 0}$$

 do geometrically?

 The eigenvalues of $A$ are

$$\lambda = \frac{\Tr(A) \pm \sqrt{\Tr(A)^2-4\det(A)}}2
 = \frac{2\pm\sqrt{4-8}}2 = 1\pm i.$$

 We choose the eigenvalue $\lambda = 1-i$ and find a corresponding eigenvector, using the [cplx-evals-22-trick]:

$$A - (1-i)I_2 = \mat{1+i -1; \star, \star}
 \;\xrightarrow{\text{eigenvector}}\;
 v = \vec{1 1+i}.$$

 According to the [cplx-diagonalization-thm], we have $A=CBC\inv$ for

$$\begin{split}
 C \amp= \mat{\Re\vec{1 1+i} \Im\vec{1 1+i}} = \mat{1 0; 1 1} \\
 B \amp= \mat{\Re(\lambda) \Im(\lambda); -\Im(\lambda) \Re(\lambda)}
 = \mat{1 -1; 1 1}.
 \end{split}$$

 The matrix $B$ is the rotation-scaling matrix in the above [cplx-evals-rot-scale-1]: it rotates counterclockwise by an angle of $45^\circ$ and scales by a factor of $\sqrt 2$. The matrix $A$ does the same thing, but with respect to the $\Re(v),\Im(v)$-coordinate system:

