 \node[right=2mm of C] {${} = A_{41}$};
\end{tikzpicture}

 Therefore,

$$(-1)^{i_1+1}\det(A_{i_11})
 = (-1)^{i_1+1}\cdot(-1)^{i_2 - i_1 - 1}\det(A_{i_21})
 = -(-1)^{i_2+1}\det(A_{i_21}).$$

 The two remaining cofactors cancel out, so $d(A) = 0$, as desired.

 It remains to show that $d(I_n) = 1$. The first is the only one nonzero term in the cofactor expansion of the identity:

$$d(I_n) = 1\cdot(-1)^{1+1}\det(I_{n-1}) = 1.$$

 This proves that $\det(A) = d(A)$, i.e., that cofactor expansion along the first column computes the determinant.

 Now we show that cofactor expansion along the $j$th column also computes the determinant. By performing $j-1$ column swaps, one can move the $j$th column of a matrix to the first column, keeping the other columns in order. For example, here we move the third column to the first, using two column swaps:

