First suppose that $A$ is a row vector an $B$ is a column vector, i.e., $m = p = 1$. Then

$$\begin{split}
 AB \amp= \mat{a_1 a_2 \cdots, a_n}\vec{b_1 b_2 \vdots, b_n}
 = a_1b_1 + a_2b_2 + \cdots + a_nb_n \\
 \amp= \mat{b_1 b_2 \cdots, b_n}\vec{a_1 a_2 \vdots, a_n}
 = B^TA^T.
 \end{split}$$

 Now we use the row-column rule for matrix multiplication. Let $r_1,r_2,\ldots,r_m$ be the rows of $A$, and let $c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_p$ be the columns of $B$, so

$$AB =
 \mat[c]{ \matrow{r_1}; \matrow{r_2}; \vdots ; \matrow{r_m}}
 \mat{| | ,, |; c_1 c_2 \cdots, c_p; | | ,, |}
 = \mat{ r_1c_1 r_1c_2 \cdots, r_1c_p;
 r_2c_1 r_2c_2 \cdots, r_2c_p;
 \vdots, \vdots, , \vdots;
 r_mc_1 r_mc_2 \cdots, r_mc_p}.$$

 By the case we handled above, we have $r_ic_j = c_j^Tr_i^T$. Then

$$\begin{split}
 (AB)^T
 \amp= \mat{ r_1c_1 r_2c_1 \cdots, r_mc_1;
 r_1c_2 r_2c_2 \cdots, r_mc_2;
 \vdots, \vdots, , \vdots;
 r_1c_p r_2c_p \cdots, r_mc_p} \\
 \amp= \mat{ c_1^Tr_1^T c_1^Tr_2^T \cdots, c_1^Tr_m^T;
 c_2^Tr_1^T c_2^Tr_2^T \cdots, c_2^Tr_m^T;
 \vdots, \vdots, , \vdots;
 c_p^Tr_1^T c_p^Tr_2^T \cdots, c_p^Tr_m^T} \\
 \amp= \mat[c]{ \matrow{c_1^T}; \matrow{c_2^T}; \vdots ; \matrow{c_p^T}}
 \mat{| | ,, |; r_1^T r_2^T \cdots, r_m^T; | | ,, |}
 = B^TA^T.
 \end{split}$$

We have

$$\vec{-1 2 3} = -\vec{1 0 0} + 2\vec{0 1 0} + 3\vec{0 0 1}.$$

 Therefore,

$$\begin{split}
 \det\amp\mat{-1 7 2; 2 -3 2; 3 1 1}
 = -\det\mat{1 7 2; 0 -3 2; 0 1 1} \\
 \amp+ 2\det\mat{0 7 2; 1 -3 2; 0 1 1}
 + 3\det\mat{0 7 2; 0 -3 2; 1 1 1}.
 \end{split}$$

 This is the basic idea behind cofactor expansions in [determinants-cofactors].

The determinant computes the volume of the following kind of geometric object.

 In other words, a parallelepiped is the set of all linear combinations of $n$ vectors with coefficients in $[0,1]$. We can draw parallelepipeds using the parallelogram law for vector addition.

 When does a parallelepiped have zero volume? This can happen only if the parallelepiped is flat, i.e., it is squashed into a lower dimension.

