The [det-is-volume] on determinants and volumes tells us that the absolute value of the determinant is the volume of a paralellepiped. This raises the question of whether the sign of the determinant has any geometric meaning.

 A $1\times 1$ matrix $A$ is just a number $\mat a$. In this case, the parallelepiped $P$ determined by its one row is just the interval $[0,a]$ if $a \geq 0$, and it is $[a,0]$ if $a\lt0$. In this case, the sign of the determinant determines whether the interval is to the left or the right of the origin.

 For a $2\times 2$ matrix with rows $v_1,v_2$, the sign of the determinant determines whether $v_2$ is counterclockwise or clockwise from $v_1$. That is, if the counterclockwise angle from $v_1$ to $v_2$ is less than $180^\circ$, then the determinant is positive; otherwise it is negative (or zero).

