 For example, if $v_1 = {a\choose b}$, then the counterclockwise rotation of $v_1$ by $90^\circ$ is $v_2 = {-b\choose a}$ by this [linear-trans-rotation-matrix], and

$$\det\mat{a b; -b a} = a^2 + b^2 > 0.$$

 On the other hand, the clockwise rotation of $v_1$ by $90^\circ$ is $b\choose -a$, and

$$\det\mat{a b; b -a} = -a^2 - b^2 \lt 0.$$

 For a $3\times 3$ matrix with rows $v_1,v_2,v_3$, the right-hand rule determines the sign of the determinant. If you point the index finger of your right hand in the direction of $v_1$ and your middle finger in the direction of $v_2$, then the determinant is positive if your thumb points roughly in the direction of $v_3$, and it is negative otherwise.

