\end{tikzpicture}

 The area of the unit circle is $\pi$, so the area of $S$ is $\pi/2$. The transformation $T$ scales areas by a factor of $|\det(A)| = |1 - 4| = 3$, so

$$\vol(T(S)) = 3\vol(S) = \frac{3\pi}2.$$

Find the area of the interior $E$ of the ellipse defined by the equation

$$\left(\frac{2x-y}{2}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{y+3x}{3}\right)^2 = 1.$$

 This ellipse is obtained from the unit circle $X^2+Y^2=1$ by the linear change of coordinates

$$\begin{split}
 X \amp= \frac{2x-y}2 \\
 Y \amp= \frac{y+3x}3.
 \end{split}$$

 In other words, if we define a linear transformation $T\colon\R^2\to\R^2$ by

$$T\vec{x y} = \vec{(2x-y)/2 (y+3x)/3},$$

 then $T{x\choose y}$ lies on the unit circle $C$ whenever $x\choose y$ lies on $E$.

\begin{tikzpicture}
 \begin{scope}[xshift=7cm]
 \draw[grid lines] (-1.5,-1.5) grid (1.5, 1.5);
 \filldraw[fill=seq-orange, fill opacity=.2, thin]
 (0,0) circle[radius=1];
 \node at (0,0) {$C$};
 \end{scope}

 \draw[->, thick] (2.1,0) to[bend left, "$T$" above=1mm]
 node[below=2mm]{$\mat{1 -1/2; 1 1/3}$} (7-2.1,0);

 \begin{scope}[xshift=0cm]
 \draw[grid lines] (-1.5,-2.5) grid (1.5, 2.5);
 \begin{scope}[cm={2/5,-6/5,3/5,6/5,(0,0)}]
 \filldraw[fill=seq-orange, fill opacity=.2, thin]
 (0,0) circle[radius=1];
 \node at (0,0) {$E$};
 \end{scope}
 \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}

 We compute the standard matrix $A$ for $T$ by evaluating on the standard coordinate vectors:

$$T\vec{1 0} = \vec{1 1} \qquad T\vec{0 1} = \vec{-1/2 1/3}
 \quad\implies\quad A = \mat{1 -1/2; 1 1/3}.$$

 Therefore, $T$ scales areas by a factor of $|\det(A)| = |\frac13+\frac12| = \frac56.$ The area of the unit circle is $\pi$, so

$$\pi = \vol(C) = \vol(T(E)) = |\det(A)|\cdot\vol(E) = \frac56\vol(E),$$

 and thus the area of $E$ is $6\pi/5$.

Let $L$ be a line through the origin in $\R^2$, and define $T\colon\R^2\to\R^2$ to be the transformation that sends a vector $x$ to the closest point on $L$ to $x$, as in the picture below.

\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, rotate=-15]
 \draw[seq-violet] (-3,-2) -- node[below right, very near start] {$L$} (3,2);
 \point[seq-red] (x) at (-3,2);
 \point (o) at (0,0);
 \draw[vector,seq-red] (o) -- node[auto,swap] {$x$} (x);
 \point[seq-blue, "$T(x)$" {below right,seq-blue}]
 (p) at (${-2.5/(1.5*1.5+1)}*(1.5,1)$);
 \draw[very thin, black!50] (p) -- (x);
 \pic[draw] {right angle=(x)--(p)--(o)};
\end{tikzpicture}

 This is an example of an orthogonal projection. We will see in [projections] that $T$ is a linear transformation; let $A$ be the matrix for $T$. Any vector on $L$ is not moved by $T$ because it is the closest point on $L$ to itself: hence it is an eigenvector of $A$ with eigenvalue $1$. Let $L^\perp$ be the line perpendicular to $L$ and passing through the origin. Any vector $x$ on $L^\perp$ is closest to the zero vector on $L$, so a (nonzero) such vector is an eigenvector of $A$ with eigenvalue $0$. (See this [evec-eg-projection] for a special case.) Since $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues, it is diagonalizable; in fact, we know from the [diagonalization-thm] that $A$ is similar to the matrix $\smallmat 1000$.

 Note that we never had to do any algebra! We know that $A$ is diagonalizable for geometric reasons.

 The line $L$ (violet) is the $1$-eigenspace of $A$, and $L^\perp$ (green) is the $0$-eigenspace. Since there are linearly independent eigenvectors, we know that $A$ is diagonalizable.

Let $A$ be a square matrix and let $\lambda$ be an eigenvalue of $A$. Then

$$1 \leq \text{(the geometric multiplicity of $\lambda$)}
 \leq \text{(the algebraic multiplicity of $\lambda$)}.$$

Find a basis of $\R^2$.

 We need to find two vectors in $\R^2$ that span $\R^2$ and are linearly independent. One such basis is $\bigl\{{1\choose 0},{0\choose 1}\bigr\}$:

They span because any vector $a\choose b$ can be written as a linear combination of ${1\choose 0},{0\choose 1}$:

$$\vec{a b} = a\vec{1 0} + b\vec{0 1}.$$

They are linearly independent: if

$$x\vec{1 0} + y\vec{0 1} = \vec{x y} = \vec{0 0}$$

 then $x=y=0$.

 This shows that the plane $\R^2$ has dimension 2.

