Solve a system of linear equations algebraically in parametric form.

 This chapter is devoted to the algebraic study of systems of linear equations and their solutions. We will learn a systematic way of solving equations of the form

$$\syseq{
 3x_1 + 4x_2 + 10x_3 + 19x_4 - 2x_5 - 3x_6 = 141;
 7x_1 + 2x_2 - 13x_3 - 7x_4 + 21x_5 + 8x_6 = 2567;
 -x_1 + 9x_2 + \frac 32x_3 + x_4 + 14x_5 + 27x_6 = 26;
 \frac 12x_1 + 4x_2 + 10x_3 + 11x_4 + 2x_5 + x_6 = -15\rlap.
 }$$

 In [systems-of-eqns], we will introduce systems of linear equations, the class of equations whose study forms the subject of linear algebra. In [row-reduction], will present a procedure, called row reduction, for finding all solutions of a system of linear equations. In [parametric-form], you will see hnow to express all solutions of a system of linear equations in a unique way using the parametric form of the general solution.

Let us recall one last time the structure of this book:

Solve the matrix equation $Ax=b$.

Solve the matrix equation $Ax=\lambda x$, where $\lambda$ is a number.

Approximately solve the matrix equation $Ax=b$.

 We have now come to the third part.

 Approximately solve the matrix equation $Ax=b.$

 Finding approximate solutions of equations generally requires computing the closest vector on a subspace to a given vector. This becomes an orthogonality problem: one needs to know which vectors are perpendicular to the subspace.

