In this section, we show how the dot product can be used to define orthogonality, i.e., when two vectors are perpendicular to each other.

 x\perp y$x$ is orthogonal to $y$
 Notation: $x\perp y$ means $x\cdot y = 0$.

 We motivate the above definition using the law of cosines in $\R^2$. In our language, the law of cosines asserts that if $x,y$ are two nonzero vectors, and if $\alpha>0$ is the angle between them, then

$$\|y-x\|^2 = \|x\|^2 + \|y\|^2 - 2\|x\|\,\|y\|\cos\alpha.$$

 \usetikzlibrary{angles}

\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=1cm]
