 We learned to solve this kind of orthogonal projection problem in [projections].

 In particular, finding a least-squares solution means solving a consistent system of linear equations. We can translate the above theorem into a recipe:

 To reiterate: once you have found a least-squares solution $\hat x$ of $Ax=b$, then $b_{\Col(A)}$ is equal to $A\hat x$.

 The reader may have noticed that we have been careful to say the least-squares solutions in the plural, and a least-squares solution using the indefinite article. This is because a least-squares solution need not be unique: indeed, if the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent, then $Ax=b_{\Col(A)}$ has infinitely many solutions. The following theorem, which gives equivalent criteria for uniqueness, is an analogue of this [projections-ATA-formula2].

Find the least-squares solutions of $Ax=b$ where:

$$A = \mat{0 1; 1 1; 2 1} \qquad b = \vec{6 0 0}.$$

 What quantity is being minimized?

 We have

$$A^TA = \mat{0 1 2; 1 1 1}\mat{0 1; 1 1; 2 1} = \mat{5 3; 3 3}$$

 and

$$A^T b = \mat{0 1 2; 1 1 1}\vec{6 0 0} = \vec{0 6}.$$

 We form an augmented matrix and row reduce:

$$\amat{5 3 0; 3 3 6} \rref \amat{1 0 -3; 0 1 5}.$$

 Therefore, the only least-squares solution is $\hat x = {-3\choose 5}.$

 This solution minimizes the distance from $A\hat x$ to $b$, i.e., the sum of the squares of the entries of $b-A\hat x = b-b_{\Col(A)} = b_{\Col(A)^\perp}$. In this case, we have

$$\|b-A\hat x\| = \left\|\vec{6 0 0} - \vec{5 2 -1}\right\|
 = \left\|\vec{1 -2 1}\right\| = \sqrt{1^2+(-2)^2+1^2} = \sqrt 6.$$

 Therefore, $b_{\Col(A)} = A\hat x$ is $\sqrt 6$ units from $b.$

 In the following picture, $v_1,v_2$ are the columns of $A$:

