Define $T\colon\R^2\to\R^3$ by the formula

$$T\vec{x y} = \vec{3x-y y x}.$$

 Find the standard matrix for $T$.

 We substitute the standard coordinate vectors into the formula defining $T$:

$$\left.\begin{aligned}
 T(e_1) \amp= T\vec{1 0} = \vec{3(1)-0 0 1} = \vec{3 0 1} \\
 T(e_2) \amp= T\vec{0 1} = \vec{3(0)-1 1 0} = \vec{-1 1 0}
\end{aligned}\right\}
\implies A = \mat{3 -1; 0 1; 1 0}.$$

Let $T\colon\R^3\to\R^3$ be the linear transformation that reflects over the $xy$-plane and then projects onto the $yz$-plane. What is the standard matrix for $T$?

 This transformation is described geometrically, in two steps. To find the columns of $A$, we need to follow the standard coordinate vectors through each of these steps.

