Let $T\colon\R^2\to\R^2$ be dilation by a factor of $3/2$: that is, $T(x) = 3/2x$. Is $T$ invertible? If so, what is $T\inv$?

 Let $U\colon\R^2\to\R^2$ be dilation by a factor of $2/3$: that is, $U(x) = 2/3x$. Then

$$T\circ U(x) = T\biggl(\frac 23x\biggr) = \frac 32\cdot\frac 23x = x$$

 and

$$U\circ T(x) = U\biggl(\frac 32x\biggr) = \frac 23\cdot\frac 32x = x.$$

 Hence $T\circ U = \Id_{\R^2}$ and $U\circ T = \Id_{\R^2}$, so $T$ is invertible, with inverse $U$. In other words, shrinking by a factor of $2/3$ undoes stretching by a factor of 3/2.

