Let $T\colon\R^2\to\R^2$ be counterclockwise rotation by $45^\circ$. Is $T$ invertible? If so, what is $T\inv$?

 Let $U\colon\R^2\to\R^2$ be clockwise rotation by $45^\circ$. Then $T\circ U$ first rotates clockwise by $45^\circ$, then counterclockwise by $45^\circ$, so the composition rotates by zero degrees: it is the identity transformation. Likewise, $U\circ T$ first rotates counterclockwise, then clockwise by the same amount, so it is the identity transformation. In other words, clockwise rotation by $45^\circ$ undoes counterclockwise rotation by $45^\circ$.

