Composition means the same thing in linear algebra as it does in Calculus. Here is the definition.

 Composing two transformations means chaining them together: $T\circ U$ is the transformation that first applies $U$, then applies $T$ (note the order of operations). More precisely, to evaluate $T\circ U$ on an input vector $x$, first you evaluate $U(x)$, then you take this output vector of $U$ and use it as an input vector of $T$: that is, $(T\circ U)(x) = T(U(x))$. Of course, this only makes sense when the outputs of $U$ are valid inputs of $T$, that is, when the range of $U$ is contained in the domain of $T$.

