 The orthogonal complement of $\R^n$ is $\{0\}$, since the zero vector is the only vector that is orthogonal to all of the vectors in $\R^n$.

 For the same reason, we have $\{0\}^\perp = \R^n$.

To justify the first equality, we need to show that a vector $x$ is perpendicular to the all of the vectors in $W$ if and only if it is perpendicular only to $v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m$. Since the $v_i$ are contained in $W$, we really only have to show that if $x\cdot v_1 = x\cdot v_2 = \cdots = x\cdot v_m = 0$, then $x$ is perpendicular to every vector $v$ in $W$. Indeed, any vector in $W$ has the form $v = c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_mv_m$ for suitable scalars $c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_m$, so

$$\begin{split}
 x\cdot v \amp= x\cdot(c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_mv_m) \\
 \amp= c_1(x\cdot v_1) + c_2(x\cdot v_2) + \cdots + c_m(x\cdot v_m) \\
 \amp= c_1(0) + c_2(0) + \cdots + c_m(0) = 0.
 \end{split}$$

 Therefore, $x$ is in $W^\perp.$

 To prove the second equality, we let

$$A = \mat{
 \matrow{v_1^T};
 \matrow{v_2^T};
 \vdots;
 \matrow{v_m^T}}.$$

 By the [matrixeq-row-column-prod], for any vector $x$ in $\R^n$ we have

$$Ax = \vec{v_1^Tx v_2^Tx \vdots, v_m^Tx}
 = \vec{v_1\cdot x v_2\cdot x \vdots, v_m\cdot x}.$$

 Therefore, $x$ is in $\Nul(A)$ if and only if $x$ is perpendicular to each vector $v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m$.

Is this set orthogonal? Is it orthonormal?

$$\cB = \left\{\vec{1 1 1},\;\vec{1 -2 1},\;\vec{1 0 -1}\right\}$$

 We check that

$$\vec{1 1 1}\cdot\vec{1 -2 1} = 0
 \qquad \vec{1 1 1}\cdot\vec{1 0 -1} = 0
 \qquad \vec{1 -2 1}\cdot\vec{1 0 -1} = 0.$$

 Therefore, $\cB$ is orthogonal.

