What is the solution set of $Ax=b$, where

$$A = \mat{1 -1 2; -2 2 -4} \sptxt{and} b = \vec{1 -2}?$$

 (Compare this [solnsets-eg-plane].)

We row reduce the associated augmented matrix:

$$\amat{1 -1 2 1; -2 2 -4 -2} \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad
 \mat{1 -1 2 1; 0 0 0 0}.$$

 This corresponds to the single equation $x_1 - x_2 + 2x_3 = 1$. We can write the parametric form as follows:

$$\syseq{x_1 = x_2 - 2x_3 + 1; x_2 = x_2 \+ \. + 0; x_3 = \. \+ x_3 + 0\rlap.}$$

 We turn the above system into a vector equation:

$$x = \vec{x_1 x_2 x_3} = x_2\vec{1 1 0} + x_3\vec{-2 0 1} + \vec{1 0 0}.$$

 This vector equation is called the parametric vector form of the solution set.
 Since $x_2$ and $x_3$ are allowed to be anything, this says that the solution set is the set of all linear combinations of $\vec{1 1 1}$ and $\vec{-2 0 1}$, translated by the vector $p = \vec{1 0 0}$. This is a plane which contains $p$ and is parallel to $\Span\left\{\vec{1 1 1},\;\vec{-2 0 1}\right\}$: it is a translate of a plane. We write the solution set as

$$\Span\left\{\vec{1 1 1},\;\vec{-2 0 1}\right\} + \vec{1 0 0}.$$

 Here is a picture of the solution set:

