Scalar Multiplication

A scalar multiple of a vector $v$ has the same (or opposite) direction, but a different length. For instance, $2v$ is the vector in the direction of $v$ but twice as long, and $-\frac 12v$ is the vector in the opposite direction of $v$, but half as long. Note that the set of all scalar multiples of a (nonzero) vector $v$ is a line.

